WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.419 --> 00:00:09.750
The “Analyze Gene List” feature can be
used to investigate a list of protein or small

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:13.840
molecule identifiers with or without expression
data.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:19.310
The tool performs over-representation and
pathway-topology analysis on the input data.

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:24.260
The submission process recognises many types
of identifiers.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:28.850
As part of the pre-analysis, they are mapped
to Reactome molecules.

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:35.520
The ideal identifiers to use are UniProt IDs
for proteins, ChEBI IDs for small molecules,

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:42.249
and either HGNC gene symbols or Ensembl IDs
for DNA/RNA molecules, as these are our main

00:00:42.249 --> 00:00:45.920
external reference sources for proteins and
small molecules.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:50.179
There 2 types of data format that can be submitted.

00:00:50.179 --> 00:00:54.989
To submit the qualitative data, the data should
be a single column of identifiers such as

00:00:54.989 --> 00:00:59.170
UniProt IDs, gene symbols or ChEBI IDs.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:05.960
They are matched to Reactome pathways and
tested for over-representation and pathway-topology.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:10.460
To submit the quantitative data, the data
should have a column of identifiers as before

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:16.210
and one or more additional columns of numbers
such as expression or dose-response values.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:20.640
This will be recognised as quantitative data
and an additional overlay process will be

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.590
run along with over-representation.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:24.590
The numbers are used to produce a coloured
overlay for Reactome pathway diagrams.

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:29.490
Selecting the Continue button on the Analysis
submission form brings you to an options page.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:34.090
Several popular proteomics tools generate
results that contain a mixture of human and

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:36.810
other species identifiers.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:41.340
When the default option of Project to human
is selected, all non-human identifiers in

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:45.720
your query are converted to their human orthologous
equivalents.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:50.710
As Reactome pathways represent human events,
this conversion will maximise the chances

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:54.040
of a successful match to a Reactome pathway.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:59.119
However, you may want to uncheck this box
if your query represents data from an infected

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:03.399
tissue, i.e. it consists of a mixture of human
and microbial identifiers.

00:02:03.399 --> 00:02:08.530
To prevent conversion of non-human identifiers
to human, uncheck the box.

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:12.560
The “Include Interactors” option is unchecked
by default.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.680
With this option selected, Reactome pathways
are expanded by including all protein-protein

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:23.150
interactors from the IntAct database for every
protein represented by the pathway.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:27.430
This can greatly increase the size of the
pathway and extend it to include proteins

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:32.870
that would not otherwise be represented, which
maximises the chances of matching your submitted

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:35.650
identifiers to the resulting expanded pathways.

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:40.819
However, the risk of this pathway expansion
is that the expanded protein set has not been

00:02:40.819 --> 00:02:44.360
verified by manual curation and review.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:48.660
You should understand that IntAct interactions
may be ‘false positives’ due to artefacts

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:53.379
resulting from the technology used with no
biological relevance.

00:02:53.379 --> 00:02:58.910
For single-column input list of protein or
small molecule identifiers, over-representation

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:02.569
and pathway-topology analyses are performed.

00:03:02.569 --> 00:03:06.680
The names of the columns are explained when
you hover over the column name on the Reactome

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:08.689
webpage.

00:03:08.689 --> 00:03:12.780
Analysis results are shown in the Analysis
tab, within the Details panel.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:18.500
All Reactome pathways are shown, in blocks
of 20 pathways, ordered by ascending false

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:24.030
discovery rate or FDR value obtained from
over-representation analysis.

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:29.060
In addition, the number of molecules matched
to total number of molecules and FDR values

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:34.540
are added to the right side of pathway names
in the Pathway hierarchy panel.

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:38.890
Reactions that contain any of the matching
molecules are boxed in orange.

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:43.310
When you click on the name of a pathway in
the Analysis tab, the Pathway hierarchy will

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:47.590
expand to show it and its name will be highlighted
in dark blue.

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:51.890
If it has sub-pathways, you can use the plus
buttons to the left of the pathway name to

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:54.959
reveal sub-pathways and reactions.

00:03:54.959 --> 00:04:00.549
Analysis results can be filtered for various
factors, including pathway size, significance

00:04:00.549 --> 00:04:05.250
threshold, p-value and species, using the
funnel shaped filter icon on the top right

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.760
corner of the Details panel.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.959
By default, all molecules are used for over-representation
analysis.

00:04:12.959 --> 00:04:18.430
Subtypes of molecule can be selected from
a drop-down list in the filter option.

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:22.910
Selecting these subsets will re-display results
that consider only the selected molecular

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:24.350
subtype.

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:29.020
Another option in the filter allows users
to ignore disease pathways for the analysis

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:32.460
as it might skew the results in some cases.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:37.320
The Analysis tab in the Details panel has
three buttons on its left.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:40.170
By default the Results button is selected.

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:44.790
Below this is the “Not found” button that
indicates the number of submitted identifiers

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:48.920
that were not successfully matched to molecules
in Reactome.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:51.610
Click on the button to produce a list.

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:55.900
If a significant portion of the identifiers
could not be matched to pathways then ether

00:04:55.900 --> 00:05:00.150
the identifiers are not compatible to use
with Reactome and you should replace them

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:04.620
with something more suitable, or the identifiers
represent molecules that are not found in

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:10.550
our pathways, but might be connected to a
known or unknown definable process.

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:15.240
Analysis results are also reflected in the
diagrams of pathways and reactions in the

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:18.290
Diagram panel above the Details panel.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:22.970
Under the Network view, each pathway in the
network view of pathways is coloured in different

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:26.660
shades of yellow according to the over-representation
significance or p-value.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:32.900
A selection bar at the bottom of the Diagram
panel will allow you to colour pathways based

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:38.120
on the coverage which reflect the number of
matched entities instead of p-value.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:42.560
Under the Voronoi view, the voronoi map view
show the same information as in the network

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:47.780
view but this view is easier for users to
visualise the results.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:53.240
Textbook-style diagrams represent analysis
results within the label for subpathways.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:57.900
The label background changes from blue to
white, a yellow band is used to indicate the

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:01.830
proportion of the pathway that is represented
in the query dataset.

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:06.770
A grey bar above the label indicates the number
of pathway entities that is represented in

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:12.370
the query dataset, the total number of entities
in the pathway, and the FDR corrected probability

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:13.940
score.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:19.340
Under the SBGN diagram, entities in the diagram
are coloured yellow if they were represented

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:21.520
in the submitted data set.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:26.180
Complexes, sets and sub-pathway icons are
coloured to represent the proportion of the

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:31.070
molecules they contain that are represented
in the submitted identifier list.

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:36.220
To examine the details of a complex or set,
hover the mouse pointer over it, a small blue

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:39.610
triangle will appear on the right side.

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:42.370
Select this to open an information panel.

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:46.660
The uppermost tab on the left size will be
selected, the molecules represented by the

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:50.910
complex or set are displayed as a table, with
a yellow shaded background if they were in

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.120
the query dataset.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:57.860
If you submit data in a format that includes
columns of numeric values, following a first

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:03.561
column of protein, compound or other suitable
identifiers such as probe IDs, the analysis

00:07:03.561 --> 00:07:06.360
tool will interpret your data as expression
data.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:11.830
The numeric values are used to colour objects
in pathway diagrams.

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:16.740
The results page is very similar to that of
a qualitative input analysis with single-column

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:21.770
list of identifiers, with additional columns
in the Analysis details.

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:26.100
These extra columns represent the submitted
expression values.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:29.660
Clicking on a pathway name launches the relevant
pathway diagram.

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:34.830
Things to look for include:
• Objects in the Pathway diagram are coloured

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:37.960
according to the numeric values submitted.

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:42.390
The gradient yellow to blue colours are based
on a scale, which is represented in a bar

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:44.050
on the right hand side.

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:48.800
• When zoomed out, objects are coloured
to reflect the average of the values submitted

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:50.910
for the molecules.

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:55.880
When zoomed in, complexes and sets will resolve
to coloured bands, representing the values

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:58.470
of individual molecules.

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:02.710
• The experiment browser toolbar is used
to step through the columns of your data,

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:05.840
such time-points or disease progression.

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:10.410
As you move across the different columns,
the pathway diagrams will re-colour to reflect

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:17.160
the new values.

